      "id": 1,
      "text": "Duncan was originally known as New Hope before changing its name to Vernonville and finally to Duncan in 1881.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        8,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duncan%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Leroy Duncan renamed the town after himself after promising to provide land for city streets during railroad celebrations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duncan%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Duncan became a site of African-American history when the Rock Hill Negro School opened here in 1881.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duncan%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Hughes Hotel opened in 1892 and became a focal point for travelers on the road between Greenville and Spartanburg.",
